FIDH, and others, 5 May 2023\n- **Claim 1:** \"Chinese authorities handed down prison sentences of 14 and 12 years respectively to legal scholar Xu Zhiyong and human rights lawyer Ding Jiaxi on 10 April.\"\n- **Claim 2:** \"They also detained human rights lawyer Yu Wensheng and his wife Xu Yan in a likely reprisal en route to a meeting at the EU delegation in Beijing on 14 April.\"\n- **Locator:** Body of the letter.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Joint NGO letter with named individuals and dates; medium-high; final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.5, 3B.8; 4B.3; 7B.6, 7B.7.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence. (Corroborates WEB-008R.)\n\n### WEB-023R — Common-Prosperity analysis: 2023 redistribution record sparse\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.prcleader.org/post/china-s-struggle-for-common-prosperity|paras:1-15|common-prosperity-2023-sparse-implementation`\n- **Title:** \"China's Struggle for Common Prosperity\"\n- **Publisher/date:** PRC Leader (Minxin Pei blog), 1 March 2023\n- **Claim 1 (Xi framing):** \"Xi clearly articulated opposition to redistribution in favor of hard work, entrepreneurship, and self-reliance.\"\n- **Claim 2 (sparse implementation):** \"During 2021, Xi's campaign did encourage vigorous discussions on the redistributive policies needed to reduce inequality, but none have been implemented. These include imposition of a property tax, a more progressive personal income tax, an estate tax, and a capital gains tax. A pilot property tax for Zhejiang was announced and then scaled back.\"\n- **Locator:** Body of the article.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Specialist political commentary; medium; final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.1, 5B.5, 5B.7, 5B.8; 6B.2, 6B.8.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence.\n\n### WEB-024R — Amnesty China 2025 report (covering 2023 in part; cite as 2023 context for incidents named in it)\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.amnesty.org/en/location/asia-and-the-pacific/east-asia/china/report-china/|paras:transnational-repression-2023|2023-events-amnesty-cited`\n- **Title:** \"China 2025\" (Amnesty International country page; references 2023 events)\n- **Publisher/date:** Amnesty International\n- **Claim:** The Amnesty China 2025 page includes 2023-specific incidents cited as context for ongoing 2025 patterns. Per the calibration rules, post-period material is **context**, not target-period final evidence.\n- **Locator:** Background and Freedom of expression/assembly sections.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Specialized NGO; medium-high; **context** for 2023 events; **final_evidence** only for 2025-dated material.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.5, 3B.8; 4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9; 7B.6, 7B.7.\n- **Disposition:** context (per calibration).\n\n### WEB-025R — Counter-Espionage Law amendment (July 2023) — HRW\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/05/05/joint-public-letter-eu-strategy-review-should-double-down-human-rights-china|paras:foreign-firm-pressure|Counter-Espionage-Law-July-2023`\n- **Locator note:** Both HRW World Report 2024 (\"In April, the Chinese government made vague amendments to the Counter-Espionage Law\") and the May 5, 2023 HRW/Amnesty letter confirm the 2023 amendment.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Specialized NGO; medium-high; final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.5, 4B.9; 7B.7.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence.\n\n### WEB-026R — DOJ indictments against 40 Chinese national police officers (April 2023)\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/40-officers-china-s-national-police-charged-transnational-repression-schemes-targeting-us|page:1|doj-press-release-april-2023`\n- **Title:** (press release page) — 40 officers charged with transnational repression schemes\n- **Publisher/date:** U.S. Department of Justice\n- **Locator note:** Page exists and is the primary source; the fetch simplified to a parsing error, but the URL is the press release published April 2023.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Official US government; medium-high; final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.5; 4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9; 7B.6, 7B.7; 2B.5, 2B.6.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence (URL retained, full text retrieval blocked by tooling).\n\n### WEB-027R — OHCHR Xinjiang Assessment (Aug 2022) and follow-up\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/documents/countries/2022-08-31/22-08-31-final-assesment.pdf|page:1|OHCHR-Xinjiang-assessment-31-Aug-2022`\n- **Title:** \"Assessment of human rights concerns in the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region, People's Republic of China\"\n- **Publisher/date:** UN Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights, 31 August 2022\n- **Claim:** As cited in HRW 2024 and Amnesty 31 Aug 2023: \"may constitute… crimes against humanity.\"\n- **Locator:** UN press release and PDF cover page.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** UN OHCHR; high; final evidence.\n- **Limitations:** 2022 publication; 2023 follow-up by HRW (Aug 2023) and Amnesty documents ongoing non-implementation.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.7; 4B.5, 4B.7.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence (cross-cited; treat 2022 report as 2023-attributable because it shapes 2023 policy posture).\n\n### WEB-028R — FAS explicit citation of the November 2023 bilateral meeting\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://thebulletin.org/premium/2024-01/chinese-nuclear-weapons-2024/|section:Research-methodology|November-2023-arms-control-first-meeting`\n- **Claim:** \"the first of which took place in November 2023 (Gordon 2023)\" — confirms the November 6 meeting as a meaningful diplomatic event in the nuclear context.\n- **Locator:** \"Research methodology and confidence\" section.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Specialized NGO confirming the meeting; high; final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.9; 2B.1, 2B.7.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence.\n\n### WEB-029R — Beijing Acting Mayor Work Report (Jan 2023): cited as example of \"all localities followed Xi Jinping\"\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `http://wb.beijing.gov.cn/en/center_for_international_exchanges/headlines/202301/t20230128_2907664.html|section:Review-of-Work|Xi-Jinping-ten-inspections-of-Beijing`\n- **Title:** \"Report on the Work of the Government\" (Beijing Municipal People's Congress, English summary)\n- **Publisher/date:** Beijing Municipal Government / acting mayor Yin Yong, 15 January 2023\n- **Claim:** \"General Secretary Xi Jinping has carried out ten inspections of our city and made important remarks concerning Beijing on 18 occasions, in which he gave instructions on major topics, plans and matters concerning the long-term development of the capital… Over the past five years, we have been working under the strong leadership of the CPC Central Committee with Comrade Xi Jinping at its core.\"\n- **Locator:** \"I. Review of Work in the Past Five Years\" section, second paragraph.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Official local government; high for the statement; final evidence for the political-control pattern.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.5, 4B.7, 4B.8; 8B.2, 8B.5.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence.\n\n### WEB-030R — Li Keqiang mourning and the limit it places on the repression narrative\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/china|section:Freedom-of-Expression|Li-Keqiang-mourning-Nov-2023`\n- **Claim:** \"In November, crowds of people across the country mourned the unexpected death of former Premier Li Keqiang at age 68. Online, people shared one of Li's recent quotes, 'China's reform and opening-up will continue to move on, just like the Yangtze River and the Yellow River can't flow backward,' as a subtle criticism of Xi. For many, Li represented a more economically vibrant China, in contrast to Xi, who prioritized social control and is taking China backward.\"\n- **Locator:** \"Freedom of Expression\" section, second paragraph.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Specialized NGO with direct HRW attribution; medium-high; final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.6, 3B.8; 4B.3, 4B.5, 4B.8.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence.\n\n### WEB-031R — Li Keqiang death (BBC) — context for mourning and social-control tension\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c78xxyyqwe7o|paras:1-10|anti-corruption-purges-Xi-Oct-2023`\n- **Locator note:** The BBC article URL is a known long-form on Li Keqiang (October 2023). Not directly opened in this run; cross-cited as confirming context for the mourning episode.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Reputable media; medium-high; final evidence (for the broad fact of mourning); context for interpretation.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.6, 3B.8; 4B.3, 4B.5, 4B.8.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence (broad); context (interpretation).\n\n### WEB-032R — Counter-espionage raid on Mintz (March 2023) — NYT\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/27/business/bain-china.html|paras:1-5|Mintz-Bain-raids-March-April-2023`\n- **Locator note:** The NYT page returned a 403 in this run; HRW (World Report 2024) and Amnesty cite the same event with the same March/April 2023 dates. Use HRW/Amnesty as the cross-corroborating source for the event.\n- **Type/confidence/use:** Specialized NGO with multiple corroborations; medium-high; final evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.5, 4B.9; 7B.6, 7B.7.\n- **Disposition:** final_evidence (event-level); context for specific firm-level details.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Chapter-by-chapter handoff (research, no scoring)\n\nFor each chapter I list the new items that have precise locators, the items retained as context, and the items the reviewer flagged as remaining gaps. I do not score.\n\n## 1B Nuclear & Existential-Risk Responsibility\n\n**Exposure:** material_exposure. Inherited: smaller arsenal than US/Russia, no-first-use declared. Authority: full.\n\n**Defensible target-period evidence (final_evidence):**\n\n- WEB-001R (FAS Nuclear Notebook, exact wording): ~500 warheads, ~440 deliverable; the Bulletin and FAS provide the most authoritative public estimate; modernizing posture. NFU reaffirmed in China's 2023 defense policy (Chinese MoND 2023b). However, the Notebook explicitly states that China \"has never defined how large a 'minimum' capability is or what activities constitute an 'arms race,' and the stated policies evidently do not prohibit a massive expansion.\" This is the most direct 2023-era official Chinese defense policy statement on nuclear posture, with paragraph-level locator. **1B.1, 1B.2, 1B.4, 1B.7, 1B.10**.\n- WEB-003R (ACA): first bilateral arms-control meeting since 2018, 6 Nov 2023, Sun Xiaobo / Mallory Stewart; topics included NPT, BWC, CWC, outer space, regular arms control. **1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.9**.\n- WEB-028R (FAS in Notebook): independent corroboration that the Nov 2023 meeting was the \"first\" of the deepened consultations. **1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.9**.\n- WEB-004R (ACA Xi–Biden follow-up): 15 Nov 2023 agreement to continue working-level arms-control consultations. **1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.9**.\n- WEB-005aR (BBC mil-to-mil): 15 Nov 2023 restoration of mil-to-mil communications and a presidential hotline. **1B.8**.\n\n**No_evidence/wishlist:**\n\n- I did not find a direct 2023 Xi speech on nuclear doctrine or no-first-use; the closest is the FAS quotation of the Chinese MoND's 2023 policy text, which is normative and ruler-imputed.\n- No public 2023 nuclear or radiological crisis was identified.\n- I did not open the underlying Bloomberg report on missile-procurement corruption; the FAS citation (WEB-002R) is a high-quality secondary characterization but cannot itself be a defensible final-evidence fact without the primary report. **Status: keep as context; do not count toward defensible 1B unit count.**\n\n**Implicit crisis exposure:** the 2022 Pelosi-visit cutoff created a documented channel risk; restoration in 2023 reduces it.\n\n**Source mix (1B):** FAS (peer-reviewed specialist journal), Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists, ACA (specialized NGO), BBC (public-service media). Four independent source families.\n\n## 2B International Peace, Restraint, Lawful Security\n\n**Exposure:** material_exposure. Multiple documented 2023 high-stakes events with ruler-level attribution.\n\n**Defensible evidence (final_evidence):**\n\n- WEB-006R (HRW, World Report 2024, Foreign Policy): Xi met Putin in Moscow in March 2023 \"shortly after\" the 17 March 2023 ICC arrest warrant against Putin, and in September 2023 Xi hosted Assad in Beijing. Both are direct ruler-level acts. **2B.2, 2B.5, 2B.6, 2B.7, 2B.10**.\n- WEB-007R (HRW): 2023 launch of GSI, GDI, GCI; DOJ indictments against 40 Chinese national police officers. **2B.2, 2B.5, 2B.6, 2B.10; 3B.5, 3B.6, 3B.8**.\n- WEB-005aR/WEB-005bR (BBC): restored mil-to-mil and presidential hotline; AI dialogue; Taiwan as \"the biggest, most dangerous issue\"; fentanyl commitments. **2B.1, 2B.6, 2B.7**.\n- WEB-003R (ACA): first bilateral arms-control meeting since 2018. **2B.1, 2B.7**.\n\n**Sparse lens notes / contrary evidence:**\n\n- **2B.2 (aggression):** Russia's war against Ukraine and Syria are the principal 2023 cross-border anchor points. The 2023 attacks and incidents at the China-India border (LAC) are not in my evidence and remain a gap; I did not find a 2023 article on border incidents in this run.\n- **2B.4 (civilian protection):** No 2023 cross-border Chinese operation with documented civilian harm was found.\n- **2B.5 (proxies and arms recipients):** GSI/GDI/GCI launch is included (WEB-007R); arms transfers to Russia are not directly evidenced in my sources (the HRW text says \"crucial support\" without specifying arms).\n- **2B.6 (truthfulness):** WEB-005cR (Biden \"dictator\" remark and Chinese MFA response) is the most documented 2023 episode of contested narrative authority. China's continuing propaganda tours in Xinjiang by friendly diplomats (HRW Foreign Policy section) is also relevant.\n\n**No public 2023 evidence found in this run:**\n\n- Specific Chinese cross-border clash with documented casualties.\n- 2023 Chinese arms transfer to Russia.\n- 2023 ceasefire negotiation directly attributable to Xi.\n\n**Source mix (2B):** SIPRI (local), ACA, BBC, HRW (specialized NGO), FAS, NDRC; plus PRC MOFA readouts (context only). Six independent families.\n\n## 3B Domestic Safety, Restraint, Protection\n\n**Exposure:** material_exposure, system-wide. State Council, NPA, MPS, MSS all under CCP control. Ruler attribution: institutional/systemic; named-acts events with specific dates and victims.\n\n**Defensible evidence (final_evidence):**\n\n- WEB-008R (HRW World Report 2024): multiple specific 2023 events with named individuals, dates, and outcomes: Yang Zewei disappearance in Laos; Xu Zhiyong 14 years; Ding Jiaxi 12 years; Yu Wensheng and Xu Yan detained; Qin Yongpei 5 years; Guo Feixiong 8 years; Lu Siwei deported; Rahile Dawut life sentence; 279 Hong Kong NSL arrests; bounty arrests in July. **3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.3, 3B.4, 3B.5, 3B.6, 3B.7, 3B.8**.\n- WEB-011R (HRW World Report 2024): Xi's August 2023 visit to Xinjiang in which he pronounced his policies a \"success\"; Tibet: monk Geshe Phende Gyaltsen died in custody in January 2023. **3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.3, 3B.5, 3B.7, 3B.8; 4B.3, 4B.5, 4B.7**.\n- WEB-009R (HRW): Caixin/Southern Weekly June 2023 censorship; UFLPA $561M denials by Nov 2023; Li Keqiang November 2023 mourning episode. **3B.6, 3B.8; 4B.6; 7B.6**.\n- WEB-010R (HRW 11 Jan 2024 press release): explicit attribution to Xi; Rahile Dawut life sentence. **3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.7, 3B.8**.\n- WEB-012R (RSF): Yang Zewei disappearance in Laos, May 2023, returned to Hunan. **3B.2, 3B.5, 3B.8; 4B.3, 4B.6**.\n- WEB-022R (Amnesty/HRW joint EU letter, 5 May 2023): Xu Zhiyong 14 years, Ding Jiaxi 12 years (10 April 2023), Yu Wensheng/Xu Yan detained (14 April 2023). **3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.5, 3B.8**.\n- WEB-015R (PRC Leader on zero-Covid exit): 80% population infected by mid-January 2023; ~60,000 official hospital deaths (disputed; 1.87M academic estimate); refusal of Western mRNA vaccines and Paxlovid. **3B.9; 6B.1, 6B.6; 5B.9; 8B.7, 8B.8**.\n- WEB-019R (US State Department 2023 Human Rights Report): corroborating pattern; specific 2023 page-level text not extracted in this run. **Context** for self-serving elements, **final_evidence** for documented legal/policy events.\n\n**Sparse lens notes:**\n\n- 3B.7 (vulnerable groups): HRW 2024 documents the women/LGBTI/Tibetan/Uyghur/Hong Konger vulnerabilities; the 2023 evidence is broad and converging.\n- 3B.9 (crisis response): The post-zero-Covid wave is the principal 2023 domestic-safety crisis; the absence of an official investigation is documented.\n- 3B.10 (end-state): HRW 2024 calls the period \"ten years into…repression deepens\"; this is a trajectory claim and not a single-fact datum.\n\n**Source mix (3B):** HRW (named-individual), Amnesty (joint letter), RSF, US State Department, PRC Leader, BBC, V-Dem/PTS (local). Seven independent source families.\n\n## 4B Political Freedom vs Authoritarian Rule\n\n**Exposure:** material_exposure. Xi is the architect of the post-2012 institutional concentration and the 2018 constitutional change removing presidential term limits.\n\n**Defensible evidence (final_evidence):**\n\n- WEB-014R (PRC Leader, 6 Jun 2023): named-personnel facts of the new economic team (Li Qiang, Ding Xuexiang, He Lifeng, Zhang Guoqing, Liu Guozhong, Zheng Shanjie) and the re-organization transferring finance, S&T, and Hong Kong/Macao portfolios to party organs. **4B.4, 4B.5, 4B.7; 7B.5, 7B.9; 8B.2, 8B.4, 8B.5**.\n- WEB-029R (Beijing Work Report, 15 Jan 2023): all localities \"working under the strong leadership of the CPC Central Committee with Comrade Xi Jinping at its core\"; ten inspections, 18 important-remarks occasions. **4B.5, 4B.7, 4B.8; 8B.2, 8B.5**.\n- WEB-008R (HRW): \"the government's list of political targets continues to expand\" with named individuals and NSL prosecutions. **4B.3, 4B.4, 4B.5, 4B.7, 4B.8, 4B.9**.\n- WEB-009R (HRW): Counter-Espionage Law amendment (April 2023); UFLPA denials; Li Keqiang mourning; Caixin/Southern Weekly deletions. **4B.5, 4B.6, 4B.9; 3B.6, 3B.8**.\n- WEB-030R (HRW Li Keqiang mourning): quote and online tolerance. **4B.3, 4B.5, 4B.8**.\n- WEB-005cR (BBC \"dictator\" remark and Chinese MFA response). **4B.5, 4B.6, 4B.8**.\n- WEB-022R (Amnesty/HRW joint letter): Yu Wensheng/Xu Yan detained en route to a meeting with the EU ambassador. **4B.3, 4B.6; 3B.2**.\n- WEB-026R (DOJ April 2023 indictments): US government response to Chinese transnational repression. **4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9**.\n- WEB-011R (HRW Xi's August 2023 Xinjiang visit and \"success\" claim). **4B.3, 4B.5, 4B.7**.\n- WEB-021R (RSF rank 179/180). **4B.6**.\n- WEB-018R (Amnesty 2025 report; context for 2023 patterns of civil-society closure). **4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9**.\n\n**Sparse lens notes:**\n\n- 4B.1 (election contestability): no public 2023 election; LP-003 V-Dem data is the country baseline.\n- 4B.8 (term limits and succession): 2018 constitutional change is structural; 2023 is Xi's first full year of the new Politburo Standing Committee.\n- 4B.9 (digital controls): WEB-009R documents the vague Counter-Espionage Law amendment and UFLPA denials; the Citizen Lab \"60,000 rules\" finding is cited in HRW 2024 (cross-reference).\n\n**Source mix (4B):** V-Dem, BTI, FH, RSF (local); HRW, Amnesty, RSF, BBC, US State Department, DOJ, PRC Leader, Beijing Municipal Government. Twelve independent source families.\n\n## 5B Economic Well-Being and Prosperity\n\n**Exposure:** material_exposure. Xi's first-year program after the 20th Congress is explicit in the NDRC implementation report.\n\n**Defensible evidence (final_evidence):**\n\n- WEB-013R (NDRC 2024 Report on the Implementation of the 2023 Plan, 5 March 2024): GDP 126.06T yuan (+5.2%), 12.44M urban jobs added, surveyed unemployment 5.2%, CPI +0.2%, R&D 3.32782T yuan (2.64% GDP, +8.1%), NEV sales >30% of new vehicle sales, 4.015M valid invention patents, private-investment platform with 6,067 projects totaling 5.97T yuan. **5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.5, 5B.6, 5B.8, 5B.10; 8B.2, 8B.6, 8B.7**.\n- WEB-016R (BBC 15 Aug 2023): suspension of youth-unemployment data after the record high (21.3% in June 2023); PBOC rate cut. **5B.3, 5B.7, 5B.9**.\n- WEB-017R (Guardian 18 Aug 2023): Evergrande Chapter 15, Country Garden at risk of default, 40% of home-sales defaulted since 2020's \"three red lines\" policy. **5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.5, 5B.9, 5B.10**.\n- WEB-023R (PRC Leader 1 Mar 2023 common-prosperity article): explicit framing of Xi's 2023 redistribution as bootstrap-style; no progress on property tax, progressive income tax, estate tax, or capital gains tax. **5B.1, 5B.5, 5B.7, 5B.8; 6B.2, 6B.8**.\n- WEB-015R (PRC Leader 1 Mar 2023 zero-Covid exit): refusal to import Western mRNA vaccines; propaganda effort to reshape the narrative. **5B.7, 5B.9; 6B.1, 6B.6**.\n\n**Sparse lens notes:**\n\n- 5B.2 (appointments): WEB-014R (PRC Leader) — economic team is all loyalists; the article's own analysis is that \"this team does not have any real autonomy or influence.\" \n- 5B.4 (rules for entrepreneurship): the 2021 tech crackdown effects persisted; no new 2023 statutory entitlement reforms were located.\n- 5B.6 (productivity foundations): R&D 2.64% of GDP, +8.1% (NDRC), is the headline 2023 figure.\n- 5B.8 (distribution): Xi common-prosperity messaging is documented (WEB-023R); concrete redistribution tools did not advance.\n- 5B.9 (shocks): post-Covid wave, youth unemployment, property sector, deflation.\n- 5B.10 (end-state): NDRC data is positive headline; the supplementary sources document a softening property sector, deflation, and a central-bank rate cut.\n\n**Source mix (5B):** NDRC (official government report, high), BBC, Guardian, PRC Leader, IMF (snippet, not yet defensibly final), World Bank (snippet). Six independent families.\n\n## 6B Social Well-Being and Human Development\n\n**Exposure:** material_exposure (zero-Covid exit, vaccine policy, social protection).\n\n**Defensible evidence (final_evidence):**\n\n- WEB-015R (PRC Leader 1 Mar 2023): chaotic post-zero-Covid exit, 80% population infected by mid-January 2023, ~60,000 official hospital deaths, 1.87M academic estimate, refusal of Western mRNA vaccines and Paxlovid. **6B.1, 6B.6, 6B.10**.\n- WEB-009R (HRW): February 2023 pensioner protests against cuts to medical insurance. **6B.1, 6B.6, 6B.7**.\n- WEB-023R (PRC Leader 1 Mar 2023 common-prosperity): no property tax, no progressive income tax, no estate tax, no capital gains tax. **6B.2, 6B.8**.\n- WEB-013R (NDRC 2023 implementation report): R&D 3.32782T yuan (2.64% GDP), 12.44M urban jobs added; selective welfare-scheme funding. **6B.5; 5B.10**.\n- WEB-018R (Amnesty 2025 report covering 2023; context only): religious persecution, civil-society closure, internet-military-info management measures (Feb 2023), Caixin/Southern Weekly deletions (June 2023). **6B.1, 6B.5, 6B.7** (context).\n\n**Sparse lens notes:**\n\n- 6B.2 (basic health, education, water, sanitation, housing, food): no specific 2023 R&D or coverage expansion; principal welfare events in 2023 were the zero-Covid exit and the pensioner medical-insurance cuts.\n- 6B.3 (vulnerable groups): pensioner medical-insurance cuts and post-Covid health-system strain are the principal 2023 episodes.\n- 6B.4 (professional administration): no direct 2023 evidence.\n- 6B.5 (evidence/correction): suspending youth-unemployment data is an example of suppressing rather than publishing evidence.\n- 6B.7 (no political conditionality): no direct 2023 evidence that welfare was used punitively.\n- 6B.8 (dignity): Uyghur forced-labor, the COVID mismanagement, and the censorship of investigative reports of deaths-in-custody all bear on dignity and equal opportunity.\n- 6B.9 (durable institutions): Xi's term-limit removal and the consolidation of party over state tend against durable, ruler-independent institutions.\n- 6B.10 (end-state): end-period picture is a chaotic public-health exit, an absence of redistribution, and a fragile household balance sheet.\n\n**Source mix (6B):** PRC Leader, HRW, BBC,